      id: 'cs-value-reference', category: 'csharp', level: 1,
      q: 'What is the difference between value types and reference types in C#?',
      keyPoints: [
        { label: 'Value types hold the data itself; reference types hold a reference', terms: ['holds the data', 'contains the data', 'reference to', 'pointer', 'address'] },
        { label: 'Copy semantics: copying a value type copies the data', terms: ['copy', 'copied', 'copies'] },
        { label: 'Examples: struct/int/bool vs class/string/array', terms: ['struct', 'int', 'class', 'string', 'array'] },
        { label: 'Heap vs stack (usually, not always)', terms: ['heap', 'stack'] },
        { label: 'Boxing when a value type is treated as object', terms: ['boxing', 'boxed', 'unboxing'] },
      ],
      model: "A value type, like an int, a bool, or a struct, holds its data directly. When you assign it to another variable, you get a copy, so changing one doesn't affect the other. A reference type, like a class, a string, or an array, holds a reference to an object that lives on the managed heap. Assigning it copies the reference, so both variables point to the same object. People usually say value types live on the stack, but that's a simplification: a value type field inside a class lives on the heap with its parent. One more thing worth mentioning is boxing: when you treat a value type as an object, the runtime wraps it in a heap object, and that has a cost, which is why generics matter for collections.",
