      model: "I reach for a struct when the type represents a single value, is small, and is logically immutable. Think of a Point, a Money amount, or something like DateTime. The benefit is that you avoid a heap allocation per instance, which reduces garbage collection pressure in hot paths. The trade-off is that structs are copied on assignment and when passed to methods, so if they get large, say beyond roughly sixteen bytes, copying can cost more than a reference. Structs also can't inherit from other structs. In modern C# I'd usually write a readonly record struct, which gives me immutability and value equality for free.",
