      model: "An interface is a contract: it says what a type can do, with no state. A class can implement as many interfaces as it wants, which is why interfaces are the natural choice for dependency injection and for mocking in tests. An abstract class is a partial implementation: it can hold fields, constructors and shared logic, and leave some members abstract for subclasses to fill in. But C# only allows single inheritance, so using an abstract class consumes your one base class slot. My rule is: model capabilities with interfaces, like IRepository or ILogger, and use an abstract class only when there's genuinely shared code and a true is-a relationship. Since C# 8 interfaces can have default implementations, but I use that sparingly, mostly for evolving public APIs without breaking implementers.",
