      model: "My approach is to catch exceptions only where I can actually do something about them: retry, fall back, or translate them into a meaningful error. Everything else should bubble up to a global handler. In ASP.NET Core that's an exception-handling middleware that logs the error with correlation data and returns a ProblemDetails response, so the client gets a consistent shape and never sees a stack trace. When I need to rethrow, I use a bare throw, not throw ex, so the original stack trace is preserved. I also avoid using exceptions for control flow: validation failures or 'not found' are expected outcomes, so I prefer returning a result type or using TryParse-style APIs; throwing is expensive and makes the happy path harder to read. And I keep exception types specific, so callers can catch, for example, a TimeoutException without swallowing everything.",
