      model: "I read the description first to understand the intent, then the tests, because they tell me what the author thinks the behavior is. Then the code, in this order of importance: is it correct, including edge cases and error paths; is it safe, anything touching auth, money or data deletion gets extra attention; does it fit the architecture we agreed on; and only then readability and naming. I try to separate blocking comments from suggestions and I say which is which, so the author knows what must change. I ask questions rather than give orders, 'what happens if the list is empty?' instead of 'this is wrong', and I point out things I liked, because reviews are also how a team shares taste. And I prefer small pull requests; if one is too big to review properly, I'll ask to split it rather than rubber-stamp it.",
