      model: "I'd start with a picture they already know. Imagine a coffee machine that has a water tank welded inside: if the tank breaks, you throw away the machine. Dependency injection is building the machine so you plug the tank in from outside. In code, instead of a class doing 'new SqlOrderRepository' inside itself, it says in its constructor 'I need an IOrderRepository', and whoever creates the class hands one in. Why bother? Two reasons: in tests I can hand in a fake repository and test the logic without a database, and in production I can swap the implementation without touching the class. Then I'd show that the DI container in ASP.NET Core just automates the handing-in: you register 'when someone asks for IOrderRepository, give them SqlOrderRepository', and the framework wires everything. I'd finish by asking them to refactor one small class with me, because people learn this by doing it, not by hearing it.",
